ML_PAGE("l4-notes", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l4\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">LECTURE 4 · COMPLETE REFERENCE</span><h2>Full Intuitive Explanation</h2><p>The complete Lecture 4 narrative, preserved in full. Use the guided lessons for a first pass; open this when a concept still isn't clicking.</p></header><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l4\" data-action=\"print\">Print this page</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l4-subjective\">Go to subjective prep</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l4-prep\">Go to MCQ prep</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l4\" id=\"l4n-toc\"><summary><strong>Reading order</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">50 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"LECTURE 4 — FEATURE ENGINEERING FOR TEXT, TIME &amp; IMAGES\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s0\" title=\"LECTURE 4 — FEATURE ENGINEERING FOR TEXT, TIME &amp; IMAGES\">Lecture overview</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A — TEXT DATA</li><li data-full=\"33. Why is text difficult?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s2\" title=\"33. Why is text difficult?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">33</span>Text to numbers</button></li><li data-full=\"34. First clean the text\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s3\" title=\"34. First clean the text\"><span class=\"toc-no\">34</span>Cleaning pipeline</button></li><li data-full=\"35. Tokenization\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s4\" title=\"35. Tokenization\"><span class=\"toc-no\">35</span>Tokenization</button></li><li data-full=\"36. Lowercasing\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s5\" title=\"36. Lowercasing\"><span class=\"toc-no\">36</span>Lowercasing</button></li><li data-full=\"37. Stopwords\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s6\" title=\"37. Stopwords\"><span class=\"toc-no\">37</span>Stopwords</button></li><li data-full=\"38. Stemming vs lemmatization\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s7\" title=\"38. Stemming vs lemmatization\"><span class=\"toc-no\">38</span>Stemming vs lemmatization</button></li><li data-full=\"39. Bag of Words — turn documents into word counts\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s8\" title=\"39. Bag of Words — turn documents into word counts\"><span class=\"toc-no\">39</span>Bag of Words</button></li><li data-full=\"40. Why is it called a “bag” of words?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s9\" title=\"40. Why is it called a “bag” of words?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">40</span>BoW ignores word order</button></li><li data-full=\"41. N-grams — retain small pieces of word order\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s10\" title=\"41. N-grams — retain small pieces of word order\"><span class=\"toc-no\">41</span>N-grams</button></li><li data-full=\"42. Why can bigrams help?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s11\" title=\"42. Why can bigrams help?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">42</span>Bigrams and negation</button></li><li data-full=\"43. Problem with raw word counts\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s12\" title=\"43. Problem with raw word counts\"><span class=\"toc-no\">43</span>Problem with raw counts</button></li><li data-full=\"44. TF-IDF intuition\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s13\" title=\"44. TF-IDF intuition\"><span class=\"toc-no\">44</span>TF-IDF idea</button></li><li data-full=\"45. TF-IDF formula\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s14\" title=\"45. TF-IDF formula\"><span class=\"toc-no\">45</span>TF-IDF formula</button></li><li data-full=\"46. Intuitive example\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s15\" title=\"46. Intuitive example\"><span class=\"toc-no\">46</span>TF-IDF example</button></li><li data-full=\"47. Document similarity\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s16\" title=\"47. Document similarity\"><span class=\"toc-no\">47</span>Cosine similarity</button></li><li data-full=\"48. Why cosine instead of raw distance?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s17\" title=\"48. Why cosine instead of raw distance?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">48</span>Why cosine</button></li><li data-full=\"49. Document clustering\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s18\" title=\"49. Document clustering\"><span class=\"toc-no\">49</span>Document clustering</button></li><li data-full=\"50. Topic modeling with LDA\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s19\" title=\"50. Topic modeling with LDA\"><span class=\"toc-no\">50</span>Topic modeling (LDA)</button></li><li data-full=\"51. Word embeddings — move from counts toward meaning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s20\" title=\"51. Word embeddings — move from counts toward meaning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">51</span>Word embeddings</button></li><li data-full=\"52. Word2Vec intuition\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s21\" title=\"52. Word2Vec intuition\"><span class=\"toc-no\">52</span>Word2Vec: CBOW and skip-gram</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B — TEMPORAL DATA</li><li data-full=\"53. A timestamp is secretly many features\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s23\" title=\"53. A timestamp is secretly many features\"><span class=\"toc-no\">53</span>Timestamp parts</button></li><li data-full=\"54. Why not feed the timestamp directly?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s24\" title=\"54. Why not feed the timestamp directly?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">54</span>Why extract date parts</button></li><li data-full=\"55. Core date extraction\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s25\" title=\"55. Core date extraction\"><span class=\"toc-no\">55</span>Date extraction code</button></li><li data-full=\"56. Time-of-day binning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s26\" title=\"56. Time-of-day binning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">56</span>Time-of-day bins</button></li><li data-full=\"57. Time zones matter\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s27\" title=\"57. Time zones matter\"><span class=\"toc-no\">57</span>Time zones and UTC</button></li><li data-full=\"58. Epoch time\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s28\" title=\"58. Epoch time\"><span class=\"toc-no\">58</span>Epoch time</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART C — IMAGE FEATURES</li><li data-full=\"59. How does a computer “see” an image?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s30\" title=\"59. How does a computer “see” an image?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">59</span>Images as numbers</button></li><li data-full=\"60. RGB images\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s31\" title=\"60. RGB images\"><span class=\"toc-no\">60</span>RGB channels</button></li><li data-full=\"61. Grayscale\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s32\" title=\"61. Grayscale\"><span class=\"toc-no\">61</span>Grayscale</button></li><li data-full=\"62. Flattening pixel data\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s33\" title=\"62. Flattening pixel data\"><span class=\"toc-no\">62</span>Flattening pixels</button></li><li data-full=\"63. Image histograms\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s34\" title=\"63. Image histograms\"><span class=\"toc-no\">63</span>Intensity histograms</button></li><li data-full=\"64. Statistical image features\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s35\" title=\"64. Statistical image features\"><span class=\"toc-no\">64</span>Image statistics</button></li><li data-full=\"65. Edge detection\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s36\" title=\"65. Edge detection\"><span class=\"toc-no\">65</span>Edge detection</button></li><li data-full=\"66. HOG — Histogram of Oriented Gradients\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s37\" title=\"66. HOG — Histogram of Oriented Gradients\"><span class=\"toc-no\">66</span>HOG</button></li><li data-full=\"67. SURF — localized keypoint features\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s38\" title=\"67. SURF — localized keypoint features\"><span class=\"toc-no\">67</span>SURF</button></li><li data-full=\"68. Visual Bag of Words — stealing an NLP idea for images\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s39\" title=\"68. Visual Bag of Words — stealing an NLP idea for images\"><span class=\"toc-no\">68</span>Visual Bag of Words</button></li><li data-full=\"69. Visual Bag of Words example\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s40\" title=\"69. Visual Bag of Words example\"><span class=\"toc-no\">69</span>VBoW example</button></li><li data-full=\"70. CNNs — automate feature engineering\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s41\" title=\"70. CNNs — automate feature engineering\"><span class=\"toc-no\">70</span>CNNs</button></li><li data-full=\"71. What do CNN layers roughly learn?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s42\" title=\"71. What do CNN layers roughly learn?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">71</span>What CNN layers learn</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART D — PUTTING LECTURES 3 &amp; 4 TOGETHER</li><li data-full=\"72. Feature engineering is really a question of representation\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s44\" title=\"72. Feature engineering is really a question of representation\"><span class=\"toc-no\">72</span>Mixed data: one machine</button></li><li data-full=\"73. The most important pipeline rule appears again\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s45\" title=\"73. The most important pipeline rule appears again\"><span class=\"toc-no\">73</span>Pipeline order</button></li><li data-full=\"74. Why split first?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s46\" title=\"74. Why split first?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">74</span>Why split first</button></li><li data-full=\"75. The five mistakes the lecture wants you to avoid\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s47\" title=\"75. The five mistakes the lecture wants you to avoid\"><span class=\"toc-no\">75</span>Five common mistakes</button></li><li data-full=\"76. The fastest way to answer feature-engineering scenarios\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s48\" title=\"76. The fastest way to answer feature-engineering scenarios\"><span class=\"toc-no\">76</span>Which technique when</button></li><li data-full=\"77. Example exam scenario\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s49\" title=\"77. Example exam scenario\"><span class=\"toc-no\">77</span>Scenario: equipment failure</button></li><li data-full=\"78. One distinction that is easy to miss: preprocessing vs feature engineering\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s50\" title=\"78. One distinction that is easy to miss: preprocessing vs feature engineering\"><span class=\"toc-no\">78</span>Preprocessing vs feature engineering</button></li><li data-full=\"79. Lecture 3 in one mental map\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s51\" title=\"79. Lecture 3 in one mental map\"><span class=\"toc-no\">79</span>Lecture 3 summary</button></li><li data-full=\"80. Lecture 4 in one mental map\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s52\" title=\"80. Lecture 4 in one mental map\"><span class=\"toc-no\">80</span>Lecture 4 summary</button></li><li data-full=\"The entire Lecture 3–4 chapter in one idea\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4n-s53\" title=\"The entire Lecture 3–4 chapter in one idea\">Chapter summary</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l4\" open><summary><div><strong>Lecture 4 — complete intuitive explanation</strong><small>Every section of the notes, with code, maths and interactive visuals.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>50 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s0\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">LECTURE 4 — FEATURE ENGINEERING FOR TEXT, TIME &amp; IMAGES</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Now feature engineering becomes much more interesting because the raw inputs aren't conveniently numeric.</p>\n<p>The whole question becomes:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How do we turn language, timestamps and pictures into mathematics?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l4n-d1\">PART A — TEXT DATA</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">33. Why is text difficult?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose you want to classify:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;The machine is operating normally.&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>A machine-learning algorithm cannot simply multiply:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;machine&quot; × &quot;normally&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>We need:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Text}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Numerical Vector}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture explicitly describes text feature engineering as turning unstructured sentences into mathematical vectors.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">34. First clean the text</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_text_pipeline\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Text preprocessing pipeline</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Type any sentence and watch each preprocessing step.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The lecture gives a typical preprocessing pipeline:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Raw Text → Tokenization → Lowercase → Remove special characters → Remove stopwords → Stemming/Lemmatization\"><span>Raw Text</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Tokenization</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Lowercase</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Remove special characters</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Remove stopwords</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Stemming/Lemmatization</span></div>\n<p>For:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;The cats are running quickly!&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>you may eventually get something resembling:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[&quot;cat&quot;, &quot;run&quot;, &quot;quick&quot;]\n</code></pre>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">35. Tokenization</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Tokenization means splitting text into units.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;Machine learning is useful&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>becomes:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[&quot;Machine&quot;, &quot;learning&quot;, &quot;is&quot;, &quot;useful&quot;]\n</code></pre>\n<p>Nothing mystical.</p>\n<p>We are breaking one large string into pieces the algorithm can work with.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">36. Lowercasing</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Without normalization:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Machine\nmachine\nMACHINE\n</code></pre>\n<p>may be treated as three separate tokens.</p>\n<p>Lowercasing turns all of them into:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">machine\n</code></pre>\n<p>assuming capitalization itself isn't important for the task.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">37. Stopwords</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Words such as:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">the\nis\nat\nand\n</code></pre>\n<p>often occur everywhere.</p>\n<p>They may add little discriminative information.</p>\n<p>So they can be removed.</p>\n<p>The lecture describes stopwords as high-frequency terms with little information value and includes their removal as part of text preprocessing.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">38. Stemming vs lemmatization</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Both try to normalize related forms.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">running\nruns\nran\n</code></pre>\n<p>might conceptually become closer to:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">run\n</code></pre>\n<p>The chapter distinguishes stemming as a more heuristic root reduction and lemmatization as a linguistically informed canonical form.</p>\n<p>For this lecture, the key idea is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Treat related word forms more consistently.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">39. Bag of Words — turn documents into word counts</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose vocabulary is:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">cat\ndog\nsky\nblue\n</code></pre>\n<p>Document:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;blue sky&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>can become:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$[0,0,1,1]$$</div>\n<p>because:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">cat = 0\ndog = 0\nsky = 1\nblue = 1\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another document:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;dog dog cat&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>becomes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$[1,2,0,0]$$</div>\n<p>That's <strong>Bag of Words</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lecture defines BoW as converting text into a numeric term-frequency vector whose dimension is based on the vocabulary of distinct words across the corpus.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Core code</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import CountVectorizer\n\nvectorizer = CountVectorizer()\nX = vectorizer.fit_transform(documents)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Again:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Document}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Word count vector}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">40. Why is it called a “bag” of words?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Because it largely throws away:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">word order\nsyntax\nsentence structure\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture explicitly states this limitation.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">dog bites man\n</code></pre>\n<p>and:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">man bites dog\n</code></pre>\n<p>contain the same words.</p>\n<p>Simple BoW can struggle to distinguish their structure.</p>\n<p>Which is, admittedly, a fairly important distinction for the man involved.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">41. N-grams — retain small pieces of word order</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Instead of using only individual words:</p>\n<h5>Unigram</h5>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">machine\nlearning\nmodel\n</code></pre>\n<h5>Bigram</h5>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">machine learning\nlearning model\n</code></pre>\n<h5>Trigram</h5>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">machine learning model\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture defines n-grams as contiguous word sequences and notes that bigrams use two words and trigrams three.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">42. Why can bigrams help?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Consider:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">good\n</code></pre>\n<p>versus:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">not good\n</code></pre>\n<p>A basic word-count representation may separate:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">not\ngood\n</code></pre>\n<p>But a bigram can preserve:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;not good&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>as one feature.</p>\n<p>The lecture specifically uses negation as an example of why n-grams can preserve useful local word order.</p>\n<p>Core idea:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">CountVectorizer(ngram_range=(1, 2))\n</code></pre>\n<p>means consider unigrams and bigrams.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">43. Problem with raw word counts</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose every document contains:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">machine\n</code></pre>\n<p>100 times.</p>\n<p>And one particular document contains:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">bearing_failure\n</code></pre>\n<p>once.</p>\n<p>Which term is more useful for identifying what that document is about?</p>\n<p>Potentially:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">bearing_failure\n</code></pre>\n<p>Yet raw count can make the frequent generic word dominate.</p>\n<p>That's where TF-IDF comes in.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">44. TF-IDF intuition</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_tfidf\"><div class=\"vis-title\">BoW, TF-IDF and cosine similarity</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Edit the documents; Bag-of-Words counts, TF-IDF weights and cosine similarities update live.</figcaption></figure></div><p>TF-IDF asks two questions.</p>\n<h5>Term Frequency — TF</h5>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How common is this word in <strong>this document</strong>?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h5>Inverse Document Frequency — IDF</h5>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How rare is this word across <strong>all documents</strong>?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecture's central idea is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Words that appear everywhere get penalized; words that distinguish particular documents get more weight.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">45. TF-IDF formula</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture gives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{TFIDF} = \\text{TF} \\times \\text{IDF}$$</div>\n<p>with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{IDF}(t)=\\log\\left(\\frac{N}{\\text{df}(t)}\\right)$$</div>\n<p>where:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$N=\\text{number of documents}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{df}(t)=\\text{number of documents containing term}\\ t$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">46. Intuitive example</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Imagine 100 documents.</p>\n<p>The word:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">the\n</code></pre>\n<p>appears in:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$100/100$$</div>\n<p>documents.</p>\n<p>Then roughly:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{IDF} \\approx \\log(1)=0$$</div>\n<p>Very little distinctive value.</p>\n<p>But:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">compressor_failure\n</code></pre>\n<p>appears in:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$2/100$$</div>\n<p>documents.</p>\n<p>Its IDF is much larger.</p>\n<p>Therefore those two documents get a strong feature signal from it.</p>\n<p>That's the entire idea.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Core code</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer\n\ntfidf = TfidfVectorizer()\nX = tfidf.fit_transform(documents)\n</code></pre>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">47. Document similarity</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_cosine\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Cosine similarity</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Cosine similarity measures the angle between document vectors, not their length.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Once documents are vectors, you can mathematically compare them.</p>\n<p>The lecture focuses on <strong>cosine similarity</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\cos(\\theta)\n=\n\\frac{A\\cdot B}\n{\\|A\\|\\|B\\|}$$</div>\n<p>Think of each document as an arrow in a huge feature space.</p>\n<p>If arrows point in similar directions:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\theta\\approx0^\\circ$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\cos(0^\\circ)=1$$</div>\n<p>high similarity.</p>\n<p>If they're about unrelated things:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\theta\\approx90^\\circ$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\cos(90^\\circ)=0$$</div>\n<p>low similarity.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">48. Why cosine instead of raw distance?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Imagine two documents:</p>\n<p>Document A:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">dog cat animal\n</code></pre>\n<p>Document B:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">dog dog cat cat animal animal\n</code></pre>\n<p>B is twice as long, but proportions are similar.</p>\n<p>Their vectors point in approximately the same direction.</p>\n<p>Cosine similarity captures that orientation.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">49. Document clustering</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Now suppose we have hundreds of documents and no labels.</p>\n<p>We can:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Documents → TFIDF vectors → Similarity/feature space → Clustering\"><span>Documents</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>TFIDF vectors</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Similarity/feature space</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Clustering</span></div>\n<p>The lecture demonstrates documents about weather naturally clustering separately from documents about animals.</p>\n<p>This connects Lecture 4 back to Lecture 1:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{No labels} + \\text{grouping}\n=\n\\text{Unsupervised Learning}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">50. Topic modeling with LDA</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Clustering asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Which documents belong together?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Topic modelling asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What hidden topics seem to exist inside the collection?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Suppose documents contain words like:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">dog\ncat\npet\nanimal\n</code></pre>\n<p>LDA might discover:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Topic 1 → Animals\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another combination:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">rain\ncloud\nsky\nweather\n</code></pre>\n<p>might become:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Topic 2 → Weather\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture describes each topic as a distribution/collection of terms, while each document can contain a mixture of topics.</p>\n<p>The topic isn't manually labelled “Animals” by the algorithm in the human sense; we inspect its important words and interpret it.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">51. Word embeddings — move from counts toward meaning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_embed3d\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Word embeddings in 3D</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>3D · A toy word-embedding space: similar words cluster, and directions encode relationships.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Bag of Words might represent:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">cat = [1,0,0,0]\ndog = [0,1,0,0]\ncar = [0,0,1,0]\n</code></pre>\n<p>Numerically, cat and dog are just as unrelated as cat and car.</p>\n<p>But semantically:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">cat ~ dog\n</code></pre>\n<p>much more than:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">cat ~ car\n</code></pre>\n<p>Word embeddings attempt to capture that.</p>\n<p>The lecture describes embeddings as learned numerical vectors where semantically similar terms tend to be close together.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">king  → [0.8, 0.7, 0.9]\nqueen → [0.8, 0.6, 0.9]\ncar   → [0.1, 0.2, 0.3]\n</code></pre>\n<p>King and queen have similar vectors.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s21\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">52. Word2Vec intuition</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture introduces two Word2Vec ideas:</p>\n<h5>CBOW</h5>\n<p>Use context to predict the word.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Context} \\rightarrow \\text{Target Word}$$</div>\n<h5>Skip-gram</h5>\n<p>Use the word to predict surrounding context.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Target Word} \\rightarrow \\text{Context}$$</div>\n<p>The clever part is that, in learning to predict words from context, the network learns useful internal vector representations.</p>\n<p>That's where the embedding comes from.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l4n-d22\">PART B — TEMPORAL DATA</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">53. A timestamp is secretly many features</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_timestamp\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Timestamp feature extraction</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · One timestamp becomes many features — and the same instant looks different in each time zone.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Consider:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">2026-09-23 14:35:22\n</code></pre>\n<p>You could derive:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Year = 2026\nMonth = 9\nDay = 23\nHour = 14\nDayOfWeek = Wednesday\nQuarter = Q3\nWeekend = False\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture emphasizes that a temporal value contains multiple date/time components that can be extracted as features.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">54. Why not feed the timestamp directly?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Because the raw timestamp:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">2026-09-23 14:35:22\n</code></pre>\n<p>doesn't explicitly tell a basic model:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>This is Wednesday afternoon.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yet that might be the important pattern.</p>\n<p>For food delivery:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">hour\nweekend\n</code></pre>\n<p>may be extremely useful.</p>\n<p>For electricity:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">hour\nday_of_week\nmonth\n</code></pre>\n<p>may matter.</p>\n<p>For retail:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Christmas season\nsalary period\nweekend\n</code></pre>\n<p>may matter.</p>\n<p>Feature engineering exposes those patterns.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s25\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">55. Core date extraction</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture lists features such as year, month, day, quarter, day-of-week and day-of-year.</p>\n<p>Core pandas:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;year&quot;] = df[&quot;date&quot;].dt.year\ndf[&quot;month&quot;] = df[&quot;date&quot;].dt.month\ndf[&quot;hour&quot;] = df[&quot;date&quot;].dt.hour\ndf[&quot;day_of_week&quot;] = df[&quot;date&quot;].dt.dayofweek\n</code></pre>\n<p>Nothing more complicated is needed to understand the principle.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s26\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">56. Time-of-day binning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Maybe:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">14:35:22\n</code></pre>\n<p>is unnecessarily precise.</p>\n<p>You may instead create:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Morning\nAfternoon\nEvening\nNight\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture shows this exact idea: extract the hour and then use binning to create time-of-day categories.</p>\n<p>This is just the numeric binning from Lecture 3 applied to time.</p>\n<p>Feature-engineering techniques aren't isolated tricks; they combine.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s27\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">57. Time zones matter</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose one server says:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Malaysia → 14:00 UTC+8\n</code></pre>\n<p>and another:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">London → 06:00 UTC+0\n</code></pre>\n<p>These may represent the <strong>same instant</strong>.</p>\n<p>If you ignore time zones, the model may think the events occurred eight hours apart.</p>\n<p>The lecture therefore stresses normalizing temporal data to a common UTC representation.</p>\n<p>This matters enormously when combining global datasets.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s28\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">58. Epoch time</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Another representation is Unix Epoch time:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Seconds elapsed since 1 January 1970 UTC.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>So a timestamp becomes one continuous number.</p>\n<p>The lecture also discusses Gregorian ordinal dates, which represent elapsed days from an early calendar origin.</p>\n<p>Why useful?</p>\n<p>Date arithmetic.</p>\n<p>Instead of manipulating:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">2025-07-11\n2026-09-23\n</code></pre>\n<p>you can calculate:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{elapsed seconds}$$</div>\n<p>or:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{elapsed days}$$</div>\n<p>very easily.</p>\n<p>The lecture explicitly mentions deriving features such as elapsed time from major events.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l4n-d29\">PART C — IMAGE FEATURES</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s30\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">59. How does a computer “see” an image?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Not as:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Cute Labrador.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>At the basic level, it sees numbers.</p>\n<p>The lecture's key principle is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>An image can be represented as a matrix of numeric pixel values.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's the starting point.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s31\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">60. RGB images</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_rgb3d\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Images as matrices in 3D</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>3D · An 8×8 image as RGB channel matrices, then grayscale, then a flattened vector.</figcaption></figure></div><p>A colour image usually has three channels:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R = \\text{Red}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$G = \\text{Green}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$B = \\text{Blue}$$</div>\n<p>If image height is $m$, width is $n$, and there are three channels:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Shape}=(m,n,3)$$</div>\n<p>The lecture describes colour images exactly this way.</p>\n<p>So one photograph is basically three matrices stacked together.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s32\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">61. Grayscale</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Sometimes colour isn't important.</p>\n<p>Then we can reduce:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$(m,n,3)$$</div>\n<p>to:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$(m,n)$$</div>\n<p>The lecture gives grayscale intensity approximately as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Y =\n0.2125R\n+\n0.7154G\n+\n0.0721B$$</div>\n<p>Meaning one intensity value replaces the three colour channels.</p>\n<p>This reduces dimensionality.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s33\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">62. Flattening pixel data</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose a grayscale image is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$3\\times3$$</div>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">0.2 0.5 0.1\n0.7 0.8 0.4\n0.3 0.5 0.9\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can flatten it:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$[0.2,0.5,0.1,0.7,0.8,0.4,0.3,0.5,0.9]$$</div>\n<p>Now it's a standard feature vector.</p>\n<p>The lecture notes that 2D pixel matrices can be flattened into 1D inputs for ML algorithms.</p>\n<p>But there's a weakness:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Flattening largely destroys spatial relationships.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The model no longer naturally knows which pixels were neighbours.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s34\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">63. Image histograms</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Instead of giving every pixel individually, we can summarize intensity.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">0–0.1 → 300 pixels\n0.1–0.2 → 500\n...\n0.8–0.9 → 2500\n</code></pre>\n<p>That creates a histogram feature vector.</p>\n<p>The lecture compares the cat and Labrador images this way, noting that the brighter dog image has more high-intensity pixels.</p>\n<p>You lose spatial information, but retain information about overall brightness distribution.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s35\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">64. Statistical image features</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Instead of thousands of pixels, summarize with statistics.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Mean}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Variance}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Skewness}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Kurtosis}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture presents these as concise descriptors of an image's intensity distribution.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Mean → overall brightness\nVariance → intensity spread/contrast\nSkewness → asymmetry\nKurtosis → concentration/peak shape\n</code></pre>\n<p>You reduce massive raw data into a handful of features.</p>\n<p>Naturally, some information is lost.</p>\n<p>That's the trade-off.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s36\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">65. Edge detection</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_edges\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Edges and HOG</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · From pixels to edges to HOG-style orientation features.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Pixels tell you brightness.</p>\n<p>Edges tell you where brightness changes sharply.</p>\n<p>Suppose an image contains:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">white object against black background\n</code></pre>\n<p>At the object's boundary:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Intensity}$$</div>\n<p>changes suddenly.</p>\n<p>An edge detector identifies those boundaries.</p>\n<p>The lecture introduces <strong>Canny edge detection</strong> as a widely used technique for identifying sharp intensity/brightness changes and areas of interest.</p>\n<p>This can expose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">shapes\nboundaries\ncontours\n</code></pre>\n<p>rather than merely raw colour.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s37\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">66. HOG — Histogram of Oriented Gradients</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>HOG looks at the directions of edges within local regions.</p>\n<p>The lecture describes HOG as a classical feature descriptor useful for things such as object detection, capturing contour, silhouette and texture information.</p>\n<p>Intuitively:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>It's not merely asking “is there an edge?”</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It's also asking:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Which way is the edge pointing?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That helps describe object shape.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s38\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">67. SURF — localized keypoint features</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Instead of describing the entire image uniformly, SURF tries to identify interesting local regions.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">corners\ndistinctive points\nlocal patterns\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then it builds feature descriptors around those regions.</p>\n<p>The lecture describes this as <strong>localized feature extraction</strong> and identifies SURF as Speeded Up Robust Features.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Don't summarize the entire photo equally. Find interesting parts and describe them.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s39\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">68. Visual Bag of Words — stealing an NLP idea for images</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This is conceptually clever.</p>\n<p>In text:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Document\n→ words\n→ word counts\n</code></pre>\n<p>In images:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Image\n→ local visual descriptors\n→ cluster descriptors\n→ &quot;visual words&quot;\n→ counts\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture explicitly presents Visual Bag of Words as applying a text-style Bag-of-Words idea to image descriptors.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s40\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">69. Visual Bag of Words example</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Imagine many octopus images.</p>\n<p>SURF finds local descriptors.</p>\n<p>Clustering discovers common patterns that we interpret roughly like:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Visual word 1 → eye-like feature\nVisual word 2 → tentacle-like feature\nVisual word 3 → sucker-like feature\n</code></pre>\n<p>One image might become:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$[1,3,5]$$</div>\n<p>meaning:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">1 eye-feature\n3 tentacle-features\n5 sucker-features\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture uses essentially this example.</p>\n<p>A complicated image is now represented by a compact numerical vector.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s41\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">70. CNNs — automate feature engineering</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l4_cnn_layers\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>A CNN replaces hand-designed extractors with layers that learn increasingly abstract features.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Traditional computer vision often looks like:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Pixels\n↓\nHuman-designed feature extraction\n↓\nHOG / SURF / etc.\n↓\nML model\n</code></pre>\n<p>Deep learning changes the philosophy.</p>\n<p>A Convolutional Neural Network can learn useful representations itself:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Pixels\n↓\nCNN layers\n↓\nlearned features\n↓\nprediction\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture presents this as the shift from manual to automated feature extraction.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s42\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">71. What do CNN layers roughly learn?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The diagram on the slides gives a nice intuition.</p>\n<p>Early layers:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">edges\nlines\n</code></pre>\n<p>Middle layers:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">shapes\ncorners\ntextures\n</code></pre>\n<p>Deeper layers:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">more complex visual structures/concepts\n</code></pre>\n<p>So the network gradually transforms raw pixels into increasingly abstract features.</p>\n<p>That's why deep learning reduced the need for some manually engineered image features.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l4n-d43\">PART D — PUTTING LECTURES 3 &amp; 4 TOGETHER</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s44\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">72. Feature engineering is really a question of representation</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l4_mixed_hub\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Each data type gets its own representation — and every transformer is fitted on training data only.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose you're predicting machine failure and have:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Temperature = 96.7382\nEquipment_Type = &quot;Pump&quot;\nMaintenance_Note = &quot;bearing making unusual noise&quot;\nTimestamp = 2026-09-23 14:35\nInspection_Photo = image\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture's equipment-failure case maps each type to a suitable feature strategy.</p>\n<p>You might transform:</p>\n<h5>Temperature</h5>\n<p>Numeric:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">round / bin / transform if appropriate\n</code></pre>\n<h5>Equipment type</h5>\n<p>Nominal:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">one-hot encoding\n</code></pre>\n<h5>Maintenance note</h5>\n<p>Text:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">TF-IDF / text features\n</code></pre>\n<h5>Timestamp</h5>\n<p>Temporal:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">month\nhour\nday of week\n</code></pre>\n<h5>Inspection photo</h5>\n<p>Image:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">visual features / CNN\n</code></pre>\n<p>Same machine.</p>\n<p>Five very different representations.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s45\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">73. The most important pipeline rule appears again</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture's end-to-end workflow is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Train/Test Split}$$</div>\n<p><strong>first</strong>, then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Fit Transformers on Train}$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Transform Train}$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Transform Test using existing transformers}$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Train Model}$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Evaluate}$$</div>\n<p>This is one of the most important recurring themes from Lecture 2 onward.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s46\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">74. Why split first?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose you log-transform using no learned parameters. Fine.</p>\n<p>But other operations learn things:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">quantile boundaries\ncategory vocabulary\nTF-IDF vocabulary\nIDF weights\nscaling means\nscaling standard deviations\n</code></pre>\n<p>If those are calculated using your test data, information from the “future” has entered training.</p>\n<p>That's leakage.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Split First}$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Fit preprocessing on train}$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Apply it elsewhere}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s47\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">75. The five mistakes the lecture wants you to avoid</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The final slides identify the major traps: treating nominal text as ordered numbers, fitting preprocessing on test data, blindly creating polynomial features, using fixed bins on highly skewed distributions, and ignoring timezone normalization.</p>\n<p>Rather than memorizing those as five facts, notice the underlying principle:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Every transformation makes an assumption about the data.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Label encoding assumes order.</p>\n<p>Fixed binning assumes useful boundaries.</p>\n<p>Polynomial expansion assumes interactions may matter.</p>\n<p>Timezone-naïve processing assumes timestamps already share a reference frame.</p>\n<p>If the assumption is wrong, feature engineering can make the data <strong>worse</strong>, not better.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s48\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">76. The fastest way to answer feature-engineering scenarios</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Use this decision process:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>You see...</th>\n<th>Think...</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Numeric count where presence matters</td>\n<td>Binarization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Meaningless decimal detail</td>\n<td>Rounding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Two variables may jointly matter</td>\n<td>Interaction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exact number less important than range</td>\n<td>Binning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strongly skewed positive values</td>\n<td>Log / Box-Cox</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nominal category</td>\n<td>One-hot / hashing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ordered category</td>\n<td>Ordinal mapping</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Text</td>\n<td>preprocess → BoW / N-gram / TF-IDF / embeddings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Timestamp</td>\n<td>extract date/time components</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Global timestamps</td>\n<td>normalize timezone/UTC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Image</td>\n<td>pixels → image descriptors or CNN</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>And then always ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Was this transformation learned only from training data?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s49\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">77. Example exam scenario</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A factory wants to predict equipment failure. Variables include <code>machine_type</code>, <code>temperature</code>, <code>maintenance_comment</code>, <code>timestamp</code>, and a thermal inspection image.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Reason systematically.</p>\n<h5><code>machine_type</code></h5>\n<p>Values:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Pump\nCompressor\nTurbine\n</code></pre>\n<p>No meaningful ranking.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Nominal}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{OneHot}$$</div>\n<hr />\n<h5><code>temperature</code></h5>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">83.283715\n</code></pre>\n<p>Maybe precision beyond one decimal is sensor noise.</p>\n<p>Potentially:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Rounding}$$</div>\n<p>If the relationship is better expressed as:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Normal\nWarm\nCritical\n</code></pre>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Binning}$$</div>\n<p>The correct choice depends on the problem.</p>\n<hr />\n<h5><code>maintenance_comment</code></h5>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;abnormal vibration near bearing&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Text.</p>\n<p>Could:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Preprocess}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{TFIDF}$$</div>\n<hr />\n<h5><code>timestamp</code></h5>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">2026-10-03 02:14:00\n</code></pre>\n<p>Possible features:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">hour = 2\nday_of_week\nmonth\nnight_shift = True\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<h5>Inspection image</h5>\n<p>Could engineer:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">pixel/intensity features\nedges\nHOG\n</code></pre>\n<p>or allow a CNN to learn visual representations.</p>\n<p>That's exactly the kind of reasoning the lecture is trying to build.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s50\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">78. One distinction that is easy to miss: preprocessing vs feature engineering</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>There's some overlap, but conceptually:</p>\n<h5>Preprocessing</h5>\n<p>Makes data usable/clean.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">fix missing values\ncorrect type\nremove bad records\nnormalize text\n</code></pre>\n<h5>Feature engineering</h5>\n<p>Makes data <strong>informative to the model</strong>.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">date → weekend\nincome → log income\nattack + defense → interaction\nreview → TF-IDF vector\nimage → edge features\n</code></pre>\n<p>A useful mental distinction is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Preprocessing}:\n\\quad\n\\text{\"Can the model safely consume this?\"}$$</div>\n<p>versus:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Feature Engineering}:\n\\quad\n\\text{\"Is this the best representation for learning the pattern?\"}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s51\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">79. Lecture 3 in one mental map</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Raw Numeric/Categorical Data}\n}$$</div>\n<p>For numbers:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow\n\\text{Binarize/Round/Interact/Bin/Transform}$$</div>\n<p>For categories:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow\n\\text{Determine Nominal vs Ordinal}$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Ordinal} \\rightarrow \\text{Ordered mapping}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Nominal} \\rightarrow \\text{OneHot/Dummy/Hash}$$</div>\n<p>The goal is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Useful numerical features without inventing false relationships}\n}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s52\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">80. Lecture 4 in one mental map</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Text:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Raw text → Clean → BoW/NGram/TFIDF/Embedding → Vector\"><span>Raw text</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Clean</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>BoW/NGram/TFIDF/Embedding</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Vector</span></div>\n<p>Temporal:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Timestamp}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Year/Month/Day/Hour/Weekend/ElapsedTime}$$</div>\n<p>Image:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Pixels}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Intensity/Edges/HOG/SURF}$$</div>\n<p>or:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Pixels → CNN → Learned Features\"><span>Pixels</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>CNN</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Learned Features</span></div>\n<p>All three are solving exactly the same underlying problem:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Complex raw information}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Mathematical representation}\n}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4n-s53\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">The entire Lecture 3–4 chapter in one idea</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The final slides essentially reduce the chapter to:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Raw Data} \\neq \\text{Useful Features}\n}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Good Representation}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Better Learning}\n}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture emphasizes that good features can let relatively simple models perform well, while poor representations can cripple sophisticated models.</p>\n<p>So don't think of feature engineering as:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“Convert strings into numbers.”</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's much too shallow.</p>\n<p>Think of it as:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>What information does the model actually need to see, and what mathematical representation makes that pattern easiest to learn?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That question connects <strong>everything</strong> in Lectures 3 and 4.</p></div></details></div></details>");
